    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d92">

                <pb facs="0222" n="130" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg130"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Jackson to Mr. Hay</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-12-04"><hi rendition="#italic">December 4,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I have just had a conversation with secretary of state for foreign affairs, who
                    stated that the politics of Germany in the extreme Orient are de facto the
                    politics of the open door, and Germany proposes to maintain this principle in
                    the future. Germany does not wish the <pb facs="0223" n="131" xml:id="pg131"/>
                    question to become the subject of controversy between the different powers
                    engaged in China. She thinks it would be advantageous for the United States
                    Government to confer with other European Governments having interests in China.
                    If the other cabinets adhere to the proposal of the United States Government,
                    Germany will raise no objection, and Germany is willing to have the Government
                    of the United States inform these other cabinets that no difficulty will come
                    from her if the other cabinets agree.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jackson</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Chargé</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
